                      "instruction":  "Answer any 5 of the following 7 questions. Each answer should be about 100-150 words.",
                      "questions":  [
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Write an essay on the duties of graduates according to Arignar Anna.",
                                            "ans":  "According to Arignar Anna, graduates have duties beyond earning a decent living. He accepts that personal advancement is necessary, but says it should not be the sole objective. University education is a privilege supported by society, including tillers and toilers who may never enjoy higher education themselves. Therefore graduates are indebted to the community. They must repay this debt through service rather than merely through money. Anna expects them to tone up society, bring light into dark alleys, sunshine into dingy places, solace to the afflicted, hope to the despondent and new life to everyone. Thus, graduates must become responsible citizens who make democracy fruitful and effective."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Explain Anna\u0027s view of universities in a democracy.",
                                            "ans":  "Anna sees universities as essential institutions in a democracy. He calls them repositories of knowledge and nursing grounds for emissaries of thought, wisdom and service. In the past, universities served royalty and the privileged, but in the democratic age their role has widened. They must trim, train, guide and lead the common man to become a responsible citizen. Anna also quotes Dr. S. Radhakrishnan, who says universities develop the true spirit of democracy, appreciation of others\u0027 points of view and adjustment of differences through discussion. For Anna, universities must produce broad-minded citizens who can shoulder responsibility."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Compare education in feudal days and in the democratic age.",
                                            "ans":  "In feudal and monarchical days, universities trained scholars and poets to serve royal courts, lords and nobles. Anna says wisdom was meant for the mansion, not for the marketplace. This means knowledge was kept away from ordinary people. Scholars worked in secluded spheres, far from the problems of the masses. In the democratic age, the role of universities is no longer cloistered or confined. They must serve the common man and prepare him for democratic responsibility. Education must reach society, shape citizens and help make democracy fruitful and effective."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How does Anna highlight the contribution of common people to higher education?",
                                            "ans":  "Anna reminds graduates that most of the money needed to maintain institutions of higher education comes from revenues collected from the community through the State. He especially mentions tillers and toilers, people who work hard with their bodies and may never enjoy the privilege of university education. They submit to discomfort so that the next generation can live better. This makes the graduate\u0027s education a social gift, not merely a personal achievement. Anna uses this argument to awaken gratitude and responsibility. Graduates must replenish the social chest through service."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Discuss the obstacles graduates may face while serving society.",
                                            "ans":  "Anna does not pretend that service is easy. He tells graduates that the world may dim their hope and disturb their determination. They may see practices that differ from the principles taught to them. Self-seekers may be enthroned, patient workers may be decried and tyranny may stare at them. Every step may become a struggle. Even people with robust optimism may feel discouraged and choose the path of ease and comfort. However, Anna reminds them that many men and women have carried on the crusade of service successfully, and graduates too must continue that legacy."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Why is \"The Convocation Address\" an inspiring speech for students?",
                                            "ans":  "\"The Convocation Address\" is inspiring because it speaks directly to young graduates at the moment they enter adult life. Anna congratulates them but also gives them a larger purpose. He tells them that education is not only for jobs or individual material advancement. It is a privilege made possible by society, and therefore it must be returned through service. He connects university learning with democracy, broad-mindedness, responsibility and social justice. His final blessing asks graduates to make their lives bright and to brighten the entire land. This gives students hope, duty and direction."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How is the speech relevant to modern society?",
                                            "ans":  "The speech is highly relevant to modern society because education still depends on collective resources. Public institutions, scholarships, libraries, roads, teachers and social systems are built by the labour and taxes of many people. Anna\u0027s idea that graduates are indebted to society is still true. In a world focused on personal success, his speech reminds educated youth to serve others. They can contribute through honest work, social service, civic responsibility, environmental care, teaching, innovation and compassion. The speech encourages students to become citizens who strengthen democracy and reduce suffering."
                                        }
                                    ]
